{
 "id": "2014-von-furnishing",
 "name": "Furnishing and Perceived Room Size",
 "description": "Method replication: the photographs are the paper's own views into its two 1:10 scale model rooms, shown on screen instead of through the viewport of the real models. Two identical wooden model rooms of a square 16 m2 room with 2.70 m ceiling height (40 x 40 x 27 cm as models, white walls and ceiling, grey floor), one empty and one furnished with light grey cuboids standing for a cupboard, a sideboard, a table and three stools, each photographed from the leftmost, default and rightmost viewing direction. Participants are randomly assigned, as in the paper's between-subjects design, to a scaling-cue condition (a silhouette of a person on the rear wall; they estimate the size of the full-scale room in metres) or a no-cue condition (they estimate the size of the model room in centimetres), and to one of two orders (furnished room first or unfurnished room first). For each room they can switch between the three views, estimate its width, depth and height and judge its spaciousness on the paper's 10-point scale from narrow (0) to spacious (9). Age and gender are asked at the end. Rebuilt from von Castell, Oberfeld & Hecht (2014), The effect of furnishing on perceived spatial dimensions and spaciousness of interior space, PLoS ONE 9(11): e113267 (Experiment 1), with the photographs cropped from its Figure 3 (CC BY 4.0). Image ids: furnished_cue_left/default/right, unfurnished_cue_..., furnished_nocue_..., unfurnished_nocue_...; group records the condition and order; estimates are keyed by room (furnished, unfurnished). Analysis as in the paper: relative estimation error per dimension, estimate / physical size - 1 (physical size 400 x 400 x 270 cm full scale, 40 x 40 x 27 cm model), compared between furnished and unfurnished rooms with repeated-measures (M)ANOVA, scaling cue and order as between-subjects factors, and the same for spaciousness; the paper (80 participants) found the furnished room perceived as higher but less spacious (dz = 0.38), both rooms more spacious when the furnished room came first, and spaciousness unaffected by the scaling cue; its virtual-reality Experiment 2 found no furnishing effect. Differences from the original study: (1) photographs on a screen instead of looking into the lit model rooms through a viewport at a fixed eye height of 16 cm (1.60 m at full scale), so the monocular / binocular viewing factor is dropped and the three views replace head movement at the viewport; (2) the instruction texts and question wording are ours (the paper gives the task and the scale anchors, narrow - spacious, in German eng - weit, but not its written instructions); (3) Experiment 2 (virtual rooms of seven sizes) is not included because its figure shows only the smallest and largest rooms; (4) the age and gender questions are ours; the paper's participants were 18-64 years and came from a region using the metric system.",
